{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Increasing shortness of breath over the last few weeks, worse in the last few days.\n- Symptoms: Progressive dyspnea (now breathless on minimal exertion, breathless around the house); dry cough (mild); bilateral ankle swelling noted over the past few days; feels a bit \"run-down\" but otherwise eating/drinking OK; no chest pain, no palpitations, no fever. Onset/duration: gradual over weeks with acute worsening in last few days. Aggravating: exertion; alleviating: sitting to catch breath.\n- Past medical history: Known heart failure; history of a \"small heart attack\" ~5 years ago.\n- Medications/allergies: Takes furosemide (dose unknown), bisoprolol, aspirin. No drug allergies reported.\n- Family/social history: Father died of MI in early 60s; mother has osteoporosis and is alive. Lives alone with two cats. Ex-smoker: ~1 pack/day for ~25 years, stopped. No alcohol. Works part-time as a daytime lorry driver.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: Doctor states chest exam, oxygen saturations and blood pressure were \"OK\" (no numeric vitals recorded). No fever. No peripheral or central cyanosis documented beyond ankle swelling.\n- Labs/imaging: None done yet. Doctor plans blood tests (FBC, renal function) and an echocardiogram. Physician recommended increasing furosemide dose (from once daily to twice daily) and follow-up in ~1 week.\n\n",
